<quiz>
<question type="stack">
  <name><text>Finding the mistake in a collinearity argument</text></name>
  <questiontext format="html">
    <text><![CDATA[<p>Otieno has \(P({@P[1]@},\,{@P[2]@},\,{@P[3]@})\), \(Q({@Q[1]@},\,{@Q[2]@},\,{@Q[3]@})\), \(R({@R[1]@},\,{@R[2]@},\,{@R[3]@})\) and \(S({@S[1]@},\,{@S[2]@},\,{@S[3]@})\). He writes:</p><p>\(\overrightarrow{PQ} = {@PQ@}\), \(\overrightarrow{RS} = {@RS@} = [[if test="is(kk = -1)"]]-[[else]]{@kk@}[[/if]]\,\overrightarrow{PQ}\). <q>So P, Q, R and S lie on one straight line.</q></p><p>(a) Which statement about his argument is correct?</p><div>[[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</div><p>(b) Find the vector \(\overrightarrow{PR}\).</p><div>\(\overrightarrow{PR} =\) [[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</div>]]></text>
  </questiontext>
  <generalfeedback format="html">
    <text><![CDATA[<p>(a) \(\overrightarrow{RS} = [[if test="is(kk = -1)"]]-[[else]]{@kk@}[[/if]]\,\overrightarrow{PQ}\), so the two vectors are parallel. That alone does not put the points on one line: parallel vectors can lie on different lines. Collinear means parallel and sharing a point.</p><p>(b) \(\overrightarrow{PR} = \mathbf{r} - \mathbf{p} = {@tPR@}\). Dividing by the components of \(\overrightarrow{PQ}\) gives \({@rt[1]@}\), \({@rt[2]@}\), \({@rt[3]@}\), which are not all equal. So \(\overrightarrow{PR}\) is not a multiple of \(\overrightarrow{PQ}\), R is not on the line PQ, and Otieno's conclusion is false.</p>]]></text>
  </generalfeedback>
  <defaultgrade>2</defaultgrade>
  <penalty>0.1</penalty>
  <hidden>0</hidden>
  <idnumber></idnumber>
  <stackversion><text>2026063000</text></stackversion>
  <questionvariables>
    <text><![CDATA[validate_numeric(ex) := block([sx], sx: ev(ex, simp), if listofvars(ex) = [] and freeof(%i, sx) then "" else "Type a number in each box, not a letter.");
cv3(v) := matrix([v[1]],[v[2]],[v[3]]);
e1: matrix([1],[0],[0]); badm: matrix([q],[1],[2]);
/* Design: find the mistake in a collinearity argument. RS = k PQ with k in {2, 3, -1, -2}; PR is never parallel to PQ, so Otieno's conclusion is false on every entry. Source: original. */
plist: [[[3,1,-4],[6,0,-6],[-4,4,-1],[-10,6,3]],[[-3,0,-2],[-1,-3,1],[-1,3,-3],[-5,9,-9]],[[-2,-4,5],[-3,-7,4],[4,1,5],[1,-8,2]],[[0,4,0],[1,3,2],[1,2,-1],[4,-1,5]],[[-2,0,-1],[-5,-1,-2],[-2,1,1],[-8,-1,-1]],[[0,2,2],[-1,4,4],[5,-3,5],[2,3,11]],[[-1,-1,-1],[-3,-4,2],[2,-1,-1],[4,2,-4]],[[-1,-3,1],[1,-4,3],[-4,4,2],[2,1,8]],[[3,4,5],[1,5,4],[-4,6,2],[-10,9,-1]],[[0,4,5],[-1,2,3],[2,-1,2],[0,-5,-2]]];
ix: 1+rand(10);
P: plist[ix][1]; Q: plist[ix][2]; R: plist[ix][3]; S: plist[ix][4];
PQ: cv3(Q-P); RS: cv3(S-R); kk: (S[1]-R[1])/(Q[1]-P[1]);
tPR: cv3(R-P); rPR: cv3(P-R); wPR: tPR+e1;
rt: [(R[1]-P[1])/(Q[1]-P[1]), (R[2]-P[2])/(Q[2]-P[2]), (R[3]-P[3])/(Q[3]-P[3])];
opts: [["A", false, "He is right: RS is a multiple of PQ, so all four points are on one line."], ["B", false, "He is wrong: RS is not a multiple of PQ."], ["C", true, "He is wrong: PQ and RS are parallel, but they need a common point. He must also check that R is on the line PQ, for example that PR is a multiple of PQ."], ["D", false, "He is wrong: vectors cannot show that points in three dimensions are collinear."]];
ta1: first(mcq_correct(opts));
]]></text>
